  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to refine the initial solution using a combination of gradient-based optimization and smarter constraint enforcement, leveraging the provided initial_h_values to escape local optima. The previous method used a simple random initialization which may not exploit the structure of the problem effectively. By starting from the initial_h_values and using a more sophisticated optimization strategy, we can better navigate the non-convex landscape. We'll use a hybrid approach combining gradient-based optimization with adaptive parameter tuning and constraint handling, ensuring the solution satisfies both the integral and bounds constraints precisely.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h as a vector of n_points samples in [0, 2], with the constraints: \n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- sum(h) = n_points / 2\n\nThe objective function is the maximum over all lags k of the integral of h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx, which is equivalent to max_k [1 - cross-correlation(h, h, k)].\n\nWe use a nonlinear optimization approach with the following elements:\n- Decision variables: the vector h of length n_points\n- Objective function: max_k [correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * dx]\n- Equality constraint: sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- Box constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n\nThe optimization is performed using the SLSQP method with a tailored stopping criterion to prioritize convergence over iterations.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe implementation proceeds through these steps:\na) Validate and scale the initial_h_values:\n   If provided, normalize the initial_h_values to satisfy the integral constraint using linear scaling.\nb) Initialize h with the scaled initial_h_values or a random guess if not provided.\nc) Define the objective function as the maximum of the cross-correlation product, computed via numpy.correlate with mode='full'.\nd) Set up the equality constraint for the integral and box constraints for bounds.\ne) Perform the optimization using SLSQP with the following parameters:\n   - maxiter=300\n   - ftol=1e-8\n   - eps=1e-8\n   - jac='2-point' for accurate gradient estimation\nf) After optimization, clip and rescale the solution to ensure 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum(h) = n_points / 2 exactly.\ng) Evaluate the final solution using the same objective function to ensure consistency.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 480 (a balance between accuracy and computational feasibility)\n- Initial guess: scaled initial_h_values if available, otherwise random\n- Optimization parameters: maxiter=300, ftol=1e-8\n- Constraint handling: explicit enforcement via equality and box constraints\n\nKey variations:\n- If initial_h_values is available, apply a small perturbation (e.g., 2% noise) to the initial guess to escape local minima\n- If no initial guess is available, use a regularized random initialization with a cosine-based pattern to distribute mass more evenly\n- For convergence monitoring, compute the objective every 10 iterations and stop if no improvement is observed for 20 consecutive iterations\n- When evaluating the best solution, reuse the final optimized parameters instead of recalculating from scratch\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- 90 seconds for initial setup and validation\n- 450 seconds for optimization\n- 60 seconds for final evaluation and cleanup\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Constraint enforcement via clipping and rescaling ensures feasibility\n- Early stopping if no improvement occurs for 20 iterations\n- Archival of the best solution found at every stage\n- Fallback to the initial_h_values if the optimization fails to converge\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- Verify 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- Confirm that sum(h) = n_points / 2 exactly\n- Ensure the objective function returns a finite value\n\nObjective consistency:\n- Re-evaluate the final candidate using the exact same computation method\n- Compare the computed C\u2085 with the initial solution to quantify improvement\n\nSanity checks:\n- Validate dimensions of all involved arrays\n- Confirm that the cross-correlation mode is set to 'full'\n- Ensure the normalization factor (dx = 2.0 / n_points) is applied consistently\n- Check that the returned solution is a numpy array of length n_points",
